MOS Persistent Cognitive Substrate (PCS)
Specification v0.1
Part V — Reliability, Integrity and Governance
Abstract

The Reliability and Governance Specification defines the mechanisms required to maintain correctness, durability, trust, and operational safety within the Persistent Cognitive Substrate.

This specification establishes how MOS protects cognitive information against:

corruption,
conflicting updates,
unauthorized modification,
storage failures,
system failures,
distributed inconsistencies.

The goal is not merely database reliability.

The goal is:

Preserving trustworthy cognition over time.

Chapter 1
Purpose

Cognition requires trust.

An intelligent system cannot safely reason over information if it cannot determine:

where information came from,
whether it changed,
whether it is reliable,
whether it is authorized,
whether it conflicts with other knowledge.

Therefore reliability is a core cognitive capability.

Chapter 2
Reliability Principles

The PCS follows these principles.

Principle 1
Never lose provenance

A memory without origin is not trustworthy.

Every persistent object SHALL maintain:

source,
creator,
transformation history,
timestamps,
confidence changes.
Principle 2
History before correction

Incorrect information SHALL not simply disappear.

The system SHALL preserve:

previous versions,
correction events,
contradiction records.

Knowledge evolves through history.

Principle 3
Fail safely

When uncertainty exists, MOS SHALL prefer:

reduced confidence,
isolation,
human review,
explicit conflict.

It SHALL NOT silently overwrite knowledge.

Principle 4
Trust is earned

Information quality SHALL be measurable.

Chapter 3
Data Integrity Model

The PCS defines three integrity levels.

Level 1 — Physical Integrity

Guarantees:

data is not corrupted,
storage is consistent,
checksums match.

Examples:

disk failure detection,
corruption checks,
replication validation.
Level 2 — Logical Integrity

Guarantees:

references remain valid,
versions are consistent,
relationships are preserved.
Level 3 — Cognitive Integrity

Guarantees:

knowledge remains explainable,
confidence remains accurate,
contradictions are detectable.
Chapter 4
Provenance Integrity

Every Cognitive Object SHALL contain provenance metadata.

Minimum:

Origin

Creator

Source Object

Creation Time

Transformation Chain

Confidence History

Validation History

Example:

User Statement

      ↓

Fact Extraction Model

      ↓

Fact Object

      ↓

Confidence Update

      ↓

Reflection Process

Every step remains traceable.

Chapter 5
Version Integrity

MOS uses immutable version chains.

Example:

Fact:

User owns Laptop


Version 1

Confidence:
0.80


↓

Version 2

Confidence:
0.95


↓

Version 3

Superseded

No version is destroyed.

Chapter 6
Contradiction Management

Contradictions are expected in cognitive systems.

They are not storage failures.

Example:

Fact A:

User lives in Stockholm

Fact B:

User moved to Gothenburg

MOS SHALL create:

Conflict Object

Fact A

Fact B

Detection Time

Evidence

Resolution Status

Possible states:

Detected

Under Review

Resolved

Accepted Multiple Truths

Invalidated
Chapter 7
Consistency Model

MOS defines multiple consistency models.

Strong Consistency

Used for:

identity,
permissions,
transactions,
security.
Eventual Consistency

Used for:

embeddings,
indexes,
summaries,
caches.
Cognitive Consistency

Used for:

beliefs,
facts,
reasoning.

Requires:

provenance,
confidence,
contradiction handling.
Chapter 8
Transaction Model

Transactions SHALL preserve cognitive coherence.

A transaction MAY include:

Create Entity

+

Create Facts

+

Create Relationships

+

Generate Embeddings

+

Update Indexes

The system SHALL distinguish:

Primary transaction

Creates authoritative knowledge.

Secondary transaction

Updates derived representations.

Chapter 9
Recovery Architecture

MOS recovery operates at multiple levels.

Object Recovery

Restore one Cognitive Object.

Domain Recovery

Restore:

Knowledge Domain,
Graph Domain,
Planning Domain.
Complete Cognitive Recovery

Restore the entire cognitive state.

Including:

memory,
relationships,
versions,
provenance.
Chapter 10
Backup Model

Backups SHALL preserve cognitive meaning.

A valid backup contains:

Objects

Versions

Relationships

Provenance

Policies

Indexes

Metadata

A backup containing only raw tables is insufficient.

Chapter 11
Disaster Recovery

MOS defines recovery objectives.

Recovery Point Objective (RPO)

How much cognitive history can be lost.

Recovery Time Objective (RTO)

How quickly cognition can be restored.

Cognitive Recovery Objective (CRO)

New MOS concept.

How much reasoning capability can be restored.

Example:

A database may recover.

But if:

embeddings are missing,
graphs are missing,
procedures are missing,

the cognitive system is impaired.

Therefore MOS measures cognitive recovery.

Chapter 12
Security Model

Security SHALL protect cognition.

The PCS defines:

Authentication

Who is accessing memory?

Authorization

What may they access?

Integrity

Who may modify knowledge?

Confidentiality

Who may observe knowledge?

Chapter 13
Cognitive Access Control

Traditional databases use row permissions.

MOS requires cognitive permissions.

Examples:

Allow:

Agent A

Read:

Market Data


Deny:

Agent A

Read:

Private User Information

Permissions SHALL apply to:

objects,
relationships,
contexts,
domains.
Chapter 14
Encryption

MOS SHALL support encryption for:

stored objects,
backups,
communication,
sensitive metadata.

Encryption policy SHALL be configurable.

Chapter 15
Audit System

Every important operation SHALL generate an audit event.

Examples:

Memory Created

Memory Modified

Fact Superseded

Permission Changed

Agent Accessed Data

Policy Updated

Audit events are immutable.

Chapter 16
Governance Model

Governance defines rules controlling cognition.

Governance Objects include:

policies,
constraints,
permissions,
approvals.

Examples:

Never expose private data

Require approval before external action

Do not execute destructive operations automatically

Governance participates in reasoning.

Chapter 17
Trust Model

Every Cognitive Object MAY contain trust metrics.

Example:

Confidence

Source Reliability

Validation Count

Usage Success Rate

Historical Accuracy

Trust evolves over time.

Chapter 18
Self-Integrity Checks

MOS SHOULD periodically perform:

orphan detection,
broken relationship detection,
duplicate detection,
contradiction analysis,
provenance validation,
confidence recalculation.

These checks are analogous to biological maintenance.

Chapter 19
Compliance

A PCS implementation complies with Part V if it provides:

provenance integrity,
version integrity,
contradiction handling,
recovery,
backup,
security,
governance,
auditing,
trust management.
